; pi4SmpWake.pi4 - MILESTONE 1 of the multi-core work: wake ONE core,
;                  have it write known values to a known address, prove
;                  it is alive there, and stop.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4SmpWake.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work/smpwake.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BENCH PROCEDURE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   pmf> deadman 15
;   pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. The
;                            --load-addr above does not pin the image.
;   pmf> receive <stage> <len> <crc>
;   pmf> run <stage>
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN. Not as a formality. This probe writes into the
; firmware's spin table and starts a second CPU; if the release protocol
; is wrong the board hangs, and the vault records that there is
; currently NO recovery path on this board that does not involve
; physical access ("Bench practice - arm the deadman"). The probe also
; re-arms the watchdog itself immediately before the release, so the
; full ~15.999 s ceiling is ahead of the dangerous instant rather than
; partly spent printing - but that is belt over braces, not instead of
; them.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ***  READ THIS BEFORE LOADING ANYTHING ELSE  ***
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IF THIS PROBE SUCCEEDS, CORE 1 IS LEFT RUNNING INSIDE THIS IMAGE, in a
; WFE park loop at an address this program prints. It returns to the
; monitor anyway, deliberately, because the monitor can then read the
; same witness bytes back with its own memory commands - a second,
; independent observer of the same evidence, written by a different
; program.
;
; But the moment you `receive` another payload to 0x400000 you overwrite
; the code core 1 is executing. There is no fault and no message; core 1
; simply starts running whatever the new image has at that spot.
;
; RESET THE BOARD BEFORE LOADING ANOTHER PAYLOAD. Let the deadman fire,
; or power-cycle. This is a rule, not a mechanism - see "THE IMAGE A
; CORE IS PARKED IN MUST NOT BE OVERWRITTEN" in RaspberryPi4/Lib/smp.pi4.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE EVIDENCE IS A ROW OF WORDS AND NOT A FLAG
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A core that starts and hangs is indistinguishable from a core that
; never started, unless it leaves evidence. A single "I am alive" flag
; is nearly the weakest evidence available: it would still be set if the
; core computed its own identity wrongly, wrote into another core's
; slot, started at the wrong exception level, or ran on a stack pointer
; nobody chose.
;
; So core 1 fills a fourteen-slot row and the primary checks the row
; against itself. The three checks that make the flag worth anything:
;
;   * the MPIDR core 1 read, masked, must equal 1
;   * the row address core 1 computed for itself must equal the one the
;     primary computes independently
;   * two slots that nothing writes must still hold the poison value
;
; and then, separately, the core must PROVE it is still alive by moving
; a counter in response to an event. That is the same discipline every
; stage of the V3D work used: bytes verified by the other side, never a
; status bit.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FOUR QUESTIONS THIS ANSWERS ON ITS FIRST RUN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Each of these has been an assumption in this tree until now, and each
; is one word in the row.
;
;   WHICH EXCEPTION LEVEL does a secondary start at?     CurrentEL slot.
;     Expected $8 - EL2 - because every core runs armstub8.S from
;     _start and erets to EL2h at :143-148. Measured, not assumed.
;
;   DOES IT INHERIT THE PRIMARY'S VECTORS?               VBAR_EL2 slot.
;     VBAR_EL2 is per-core and the stub never writes it, so the answer
;     should be "no, and it holds whatever it held out of reset". If
;     that slot ever comes back holding fault.pi4's table address, the
;     register is shared on this part and a whole paragraph of
;     RaspberryPi4/Lib/smp.pi4 needs rewriting.
;
;   WHAT STACK POINTER does the stub leave behind?       SP slot,
;     recorded BEFORE the trampoline sets its own. This is the direct
;     evidence for why every core needs its own stack. Whatever it
;     prints, it is not a stack.
;
;   IS CNTFRQ_EL0 THE SAME ON EVERY CORE?                CNTFRQ slot.
;     armstub8.S:110-112 sets it per core. If cores disagree, every
;     timing routine in the tree is wrong on three quarters of the
;     machine. Expected 54000000 ($337F980), armstub8.S:53-54.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE OUTCOMES MEAN - read this BEFORE running it
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   A  every line prints, VERDICT: CORE 1 IS AWAKE AND PARKED, prompt
;      back. Milestone 1 and milestone 2 both pass. Report the row.
;
;   B  "REFUSED" before any write. The probe checked something and did
;      not like it. Nothing was written to the spin table, nothing was
;      started, the board is exactly as it was. The line names which
;      check. This is a result, not a failure of the bench.
;
;   C  the release happens and CORE 1 NEVER ARRIVED. The mailbox write
;      landed (the probe reads it back and prints it) but no magic came
;      home. Core 1 branched somewhere and died silently, or it is not
;      polling that address. Nothing was petted during the wait, so the
;      deadman is still counting; the board resets in a few seconds and
;      Anvil comes back. Report the mailbox read-back and the row - a
;      partly-filled row says exactly how far it got.
;
;   D  arrival, but "ROW INCONSISTENT". Worse than C and more
;      interesting: something ran and wrote, but not the thing we think.
;      Report all sixteen slots.
;
;   E  nothing at all. Not an SMP finding - that is the UART or the
;      transfer, the same as for any probe in this directory.
;
; The probe cannot hang the primary core: every wait in it is bounded
; and none of them pet the watchdog. It CAN leave core 1 hung, which is
; outcome C, and a hung secondary with a healthy primary is precisely
; the state the deadman does not cover - which is why this probe stops
; arming it once the release has happened.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/smp.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CORE 1'S STACK.
;
;  Sixteen kilobytes out of this image's own BSS. Not an address picked
;  off the memory map, and that is the point: a Global array cannot
;  collide with the monitor, with the payload window's edges, with the
;  primary's stack corridor at $07F00000, or with the gap between the
;  DRAM banks, because the linker placed it inside this image's own
;  space. SmpSetStack() checks all of those anyway.
;
;  Declared as quads, not bytes. RaspberryPi4/Lib/fault.pi4:610-628
;  records why in full: the emitter lays BSS out as one `.space` per
;  Global with nothing between them, so a byte array of awkward length
;  misaligns every Global declared after it - and the array declared
;  after it here is the witness row, whose every access from core 1 is
;  a 64-bit store with no vector table to catch an alignment fault.
;
;  2048 quads = 16384 bytes. The number of quads is what is written,
;  because that is what the declaration takes; the byte count is
;  computed below rather than written twice.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PROBE_STACK_QUADS = 2048

Global Dim probe_core1Stack.i[#PROBE_STACK_QUADS]

; How many times to poll for arrival. Iterations, not time. Core 1 has
; about twenty instructions to execute between the event and the magic
; landing, so this is four orders of magnitude of headroom - large
; enough that a timeout means "it is not coming", small enough that the
; deadman still has most of its window left afterwards.
#PROBE_SPINS = 2000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PutHex16 - a full 64-bit value, all sixteen digits, always.
;
;  No suppression of leading zeroes. A register printed as "30C50830"
;  and one printed as "0000000030C50830" carry different information:
;  the second says the top half was read and was zero. That distinction
;  is the 64-bit trap RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4:1309-1314 records,
;  where an `ldr w9` instead of `ldr x9` silently loses the top half of
;  an address - and this probe prints addresses that came out of
;  another core's arithmetic, which is exactly where it would bite.
;
;  No character literals - house rule. 48 is the code for the digit
;  zero, 55 is the code for the letter A minus ten.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Row(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)                 ; the dollar sign that marks hex here
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Say(*s)
  UartWriteStr(*s)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Refused(what) - one line for a refusal, with the library's own text.
;
;  The library's message rather than a local one, so there is exactly
;  one wording per refusal in the tree and a grep for it finds the code
;  that raised it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Refused(*what)
  UartWriteStr("REFUSED at ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(SmpErrorText())
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  NegativeControls() - prove the refusals refuse, on the silicon.
;
;  Four deliberate mistakes, made on purpose, BEFORE anything is
;  written to the spin table. Each must be refused, and each prints the
;  code it was refused with.
;
;  This is not padding. A gate can check these against a model; only
;  the board can check them against the part, and they cost nothing
;  because every one of them stops before it does anything. If one of
;  these is ACCEPTED, the probe stops - a library whose guards do not
;  guard must not then be trusted to write into the firmware's memory.
;
;  Returns 1 if all four refused correctly.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NegativeControls()
  Define ok.i
  Define base.i

  ok = 1
  base = @probe_core1Stack[0]

  Say("negative controls - four deliberate mistakes:")

  ; 1. core 0 is not in the spin table at all. armstub8.S:153 sends
  ;    Aff0 = 0 to primary_cpu, so nothing ever polls $D8.
  If SmpRelease(0) <> 0
    Say("  FAIL: releasing core 0 was ACCEPTED")
    ok = 0
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  core 0 refused, code ")
    PrintDec(SmpErr())
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  ; 2. a core with no stack. This is hazard 1 and it is the one that
  ;    fails later, elsewhere, under load - so it is refused up front.
  If SmpRelease(1) <> 0
    Say("  FAIL: releasing core 1 with no stack was ACCEPTED")
    ok = 0
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  core 1 with no stack refused, code ")
    PrintDec(SmpErr())
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  ; 3. a stack too small to be one.
  If SmpSetStack(1, base, 64) <> 0
    Say("  FAIL: a 64-byte stack was ACCEPTED")
    ok = 0
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  64-byte stack refused, code ")
    PrintDec(SmpErr())
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  ; 4. a stack inside the peripheral aperture. memory.pi4:214-215: an
  ;    arena there writes to devices. A stack there writes to devices
  ;    every time anything is called.
  If SmpSetStack(1, $FE000000, 16384) <> 0
    Say("  FAIL: a stack in the peripheral aperture was ACCEPTED")
    ok = 0
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  stack at $FE000000 refused, code ")
    PrintDec(SmpErr())
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  UartWriteNl()
  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PrintRow(core) - every slot core 1 wrote, labelled, in the order the
;  trampoline wrote them.
;
;  All sixteen, including the two spares that must still be poison.
;  Printing a slot that is expected to be unchanged is not waste: it is
;  the control, and it is the only thing that would catch a store
;  landing outside its row.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PrintRow(core.i)
  Row("  magic      ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_MAGIC))
  Row("  MPIDR_EL1  ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_MPIDR))
  Row("  CurrentEL  ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_EL))
  Row("  SCTLR_EL2  ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_SCTLR))
  Row("  VBAR_EL2   ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_VBAR))
  Row("  SP as found", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_SP))
  Row("  DAIF       ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_DAIF))
  Row("  CNTFRQ_EL0 ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_CNTFRQ))
  Row("  stack top  ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_STACK))
  Row("  heartbeat  ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_HEART))
  Row("  recall     ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_RECALL))
  Row("  left       ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_LEFT))
  Row("  row addr   ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_ROW))
  Row("  no-stack   ", SmpWitness(core, #SMP_W_NOSTACK))
  Row("  spare 14   ", SmpWitness(core, 14))
  Row("  spare 15   ", SmpWitness(core, 15))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define base.i
  Define bytes.i
  Define entry.i
  Define box.i
  Define beat.i

  UartInit(115200)

  ; Belt over the monitor's braces. Re-armed again just before the
  ; release, which is the instruction that can cost a power cycle.
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  Say("pi4SmpWake - milestone 1: wake core 1 and prove it")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  PRE-FLIGHT. Everything here is a read.
  ; ==================================================================
  Row("this core MPIDR ", SmpMpidr())
  UartWriteStr("this core id    ")
  PrintDec(SmpCoreId())
  UartWriteNl()
  Row("CurrentEL raw   ", MmuCurrentEl())
  Row("SCTLR_EL2       ", MmuSctlrEl2())
  UartWriteStr("MMU enabled     ")
  PrintDec(MmuEnabled())
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; The first page, as found. This is the evidence that the spin table
  ; is where and what we think it is, and it is read BEFORE anything is
  ; written so a later re-read means something.
  Say("the firmware spin table, as found:")
  Row("  $D8 cpu0      ", SmpMailbox(0))
  Row("  $E0 cpu1      ", SmpMailbox(1))
  Row("  $E8 cpu2      ", SmpMailbox(2))
  Row("  $F0 cpu3      ", SmpMailbox(3))
  Row("  $F8 dtb_ptr32 ", SmpStubDtbPtr())
  Row("  $FC kernel    ", SmpStubKernelEntry())
  UartWriteNl()

  ; The three secondary slots must be zero. Non-zero means a core is
  ; already running something, or the first page is not the stub - and
  ; in both cases writing there is wrong. stub_magic is deliberately
  ; NOT looked for: it lives at $F0, which is core 3's mailbox, and the
  ; firmware clears it (armstub8.S:190-196).
  If SmpSpinTableQuiet() = 0
    Say("REFUSED before any write:")
    Say("  a secondary mailbox is not zero. Either a core is already")
    Say("  running, or address 0 is not the firmware stub. Nothing")
    Say("  has been written. Report the four values above.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  If SmpInit() = 0
    Refused("SmpInit")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  entry = SmpEntryAddr()
  Row("trampoline entry", entry)
  Row("witness row 1 at", @smp_witness[0] + 1 * #SMP_W_STRIDE)
  UartWriteNl()
  Say("Those two addresses are worth writing down. The monitor can")
  Say("read the witness row back itself once this returns, which is a")
  Say("second observer of the same bytes.")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  NEGATIVE CONTROLS
  ; ==================================================================
  If NegativeControls() = 0
    Say("STOPPING. A guard that does not guard must not be trusted to")
    Say("write into the firmware's memory. Nothing was released.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  THE STACK
  ; ==================================================================
  base  = @probe_core1Stack[0]
  bytes = #PROBE_STACK_QUADS * 8

  If SmpSetStack(1, base, bytes) = 0
    Refused("SmpSetStack")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Row("core 1 stack lo ", SmpStackBase(1))
  Row("core 1 stack top", SmpStackTop(1))
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  THE RELEASE
  ; ==================================================================
  ; Re-armed here so the whole ~15.999 s window is ahead of the write
  ; rather than partly spent on the printing above.
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  Say("RELEASING CORE 1 NOW.")
  UartDrain()

  If SmpRelease(1) = 0
    Refused("SmpRelease")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; Read the mailbox straight back. This is the cheapest proof that the
  ; write landed where it was aimed, and it is the one number that
  ; distinguishes "the write went astray" from "the core did not run".
  box = SmpMailbox(1)
  Row("mailbox reads   ", box)
  If box <> entry
    Say("WRONG: the mailbox does not hold the entry address. The write")
    Say("did not land. Do not read anything below as a core result.")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  DID IT ARRIVE?
  ; ==================================================================
  ; Bounded, and nothing is petted while we wait. Read hazard 4 in
  ; smp.pi4: a primary that waits forever on a wedged secondary while
  ; feeding the watchdog is the one state this board cannot be
  ; recovered from without hands.
  If SmpWaitArrived(1, #PROBE_SPINS) = 0
    Say("CORE 1 NEVER ARRIVED.")
    Say("The row below is whatever it managed before it stopped; a")
    Say("slot still holding the poison $5EEDFACE5EEDFACE is an")
    Say("instruction that never executed.")
    UartWriteNl()
    PrintRow(1)
    UartWriteNl()
    Say("Nothing was petted during the wait, so the deadman is still")
    Say("counting and the board will reset shortly.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Say("CORE 1 ARRIVED. Its row:")
  PrintRow(1)
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  IS THE ROW ITS OWN?
  ; ==================================================================
  If SmpRowConsistent(1) = 0
    Say("ROW INCONSISTENT. Something ran and wrote, but the row does")
    Say("not check out against itself. One of these is false:")
    Say("  the MPIDR it read, masked, equals 1")
    Say("  the row address it computed equals the one computed here")
    Say("  slots 14 and 15 are still poison")
    Say("Report all sixteen slots. Do not treat this as a pass.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Say("row is self-consistent: right core, right row, no overspill.")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  IS IT STILL ALIVE?
  ; ==================================================================
  ; Arrival is history. This asks the present tense: send an event and
  ; watch the park loop's counter move. A core hung anywhere - including
  ; hung inside the park loop, including gone to a vector table it does
  ; not have - cannot move it.
  beat = SmpHeartbeat(1)
  Row("heartbeat before", beat)
  If SmpProveParked(1, #PROBE_SPINS) = 0
    Say("CORE 1 IS NOT RESPONDING TO EVENTS.")
    Say("It arrived and wrote a complete row, and it is not moving now.")
    Say("That is a hang AFTER arrival - the most useful failure this")
    Say("probe can produce, because the row says exactly what state it")
    Say("was in when it got there.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Row("heartbeat after ", SmpHeartbeat(1))
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  RECALL - and the second, independent proof
  ; ==================================================================
  ; Asking core 1 to leave the park loop is worth doing here for a
  ; reason that has nothing to do with recall being a feature: on its
  ; way out, core 1 CLEARS ITS OWN MAILBOX. That mailbox lives in
  ; firmware-owned memory at $E0, outside this image, and nothing on
  ; the primary touches it. So watching it go from the entry address
  ; back to zero is a second proof that core 1 executed our code -
  ; written by core 1, in a place the primary cannot have written by
  ; accident, and it is a different word from the one the arrival test
  ; used.
  ;
  ; It also leaves the machine in the only tidy state available: core 1
  ; waiting on a clear mailbox, releasable again without a board reset.
  Say("recalling core 1 from the park loop:")
  If SmpRecall(1, #PROBE_SPINS) = 0
    Say("  CORE 1 DID NOT LEAVE THE PARK LOOP.")
    Say("  It is still parked, which is not dangerous, but recall does")
    Say("  not work and the mailbox below will still hold the entry.")
    Row("  mailbox now   ", SmpMailbox(1))
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Row("  left flag     ", SmpLeft(1))

  ; The mailbox clear happens a few instructions after the left flag, so
  ; it is polled rather than read once.
  box = 0
  For beat = 1 To #PROBE_SPINS
    box = SmpMailbox(1)
    If box = 0
      Break
    EndIf
  Next
  Row("  mailbox now   ", box)
  If box <> 0
    Say("  the left flag is set but the mailbox is still not clear.")
    Say("  Core 1 is between the two writes, or it stopped between")
    Say("  them. Report both numbers.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Say("  core 1 cleared its own mailbox. Second, independent proof:")
  Say("  that word is in firmware memory and nothing here wrote it.")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  THE VERDICT
  ; ==================================================================
  Say("VERDICT: CORE 1 WOKE, PARKED, ANSWERED, AND STOOD DOWN.")
  Say("  It ran our code, recorded its own state, installed its own")
  Say("  stack, answered an event, and cleared its own mailbox on the")
  Say("  way out. It is now idling on that mailbox, releasable again")
  Say("  without a reset. Milestone 1 and milestone 2, both.")
  UartWriteNl()
  Say("  What it is NOT: it has no exception vectors of its own, it")
  Say("  has done no work, and nothing here says two cores can share")
  Say("  data safely. Those are separate questions.")
  UartWriteNl()
  Say("*** CORE 1 IS STILL RUNNING INSIDE THIS IMAGE. ***")
  Say("Reset the board before loading another payload. Overwriting")
  Say("0x400000 while core 1 is parked in it replaces the code under a")
  Say("running CPU, with no fault and no message.")
  UartWriteNl()

  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
